# Every text file is LF in the index and LF in the working tree.
#
# Copy this into the project root unchanged, then add project-specific binary types at
# the bottom. Do not narrow the catch-all to a list of extensions: the failure this
# prevents is precisely the file type nobody thought to name.
#
# What goes wrong without it. On Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` is commonly set in the
# *system* gitconfig, so it applies to every repo on the machine and does not show up in
# any repo's own config. Git then checks text files out as CRLF while the index keeps
# LF. Two consequences that both look like something else:
#
#   1. `scripts/check_context_budget.py` measures bytes on disk, which is correct. With
#      a CRLF working copy it reads one byte per line more than CI reads from the LF
#      blob, so the same committed `max_bytes` means two different things on the two
#      sides of the gate, and the local check fails before CI would. Measured on
#      web-auditor: a ~400-byte gap on a 398-line CLAUDE.md.
#   2. A shell script or git hook checked out CRLF fails on Linux with an error that
#      names neither line endings nor the real cause.
#
# `scripts/check_environment.py` enforces the result of this file on every push.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats must never be line-ending converted or diffed as text. Extend for the
# project - archives, media, model weights, compiled artefacts, lockfiles that are not
# text.
*.png   -text
*.jpg   -text
*.jpeg  -text
*.gif   -text
*.webp  -text
*.ico   -text
*.pdf   -text
*.zip   -text
*.gz    -text
*.tar   -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf   -text
*.otf   -text
*.pyc   -text

# Office documents are zip containers. `text=auto` detects them as binary, but
# saying so explicitly costs nothing and survives someone narrowing the catch-all.
*.docx  -text
*.xlsx  -text
*.pptx  -text
*.so    -text
*.dll   -text
